<Window xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        x:Class="CircuitRF.Ui.Views.Dialogs.CopyWorkspaceDialog"
        Title="Copy a Workspace Here"
        Width="620"
        SizeToContent="Height"
        MinHeight="280"
        CanResize="False"
        WindowStartupLocation="CenterOwner"
        ShowInTaskbar="False">

    <!--
      RC-9 R-rc9-1. A workspace, opened in one action, from wherever git can reach it.

      THREE THINGS THIS DIALOG IS BUILT AROUND:

        1. TWO FIELDS AND NO OTHERS. Nothing here is host-specific (R-rc9-4): there is no account,
           no provider list, no "sign in" button, and no URL parsing beyond what git itself accepts.
           Whatever the maintainer of the library gave the designer is what goes in the first box.

        2. THE DESTINATION IS NAMED, NEVER DERIVED. git would happily work a folder name out of the
           address; circuitRF will not, because a folder appearing somewhere the designer did not
           choose is the surprise §0 forbids. Browse fills it in; typing over it is fine.

        3. THE ROW THAT SAYS WHAT DOES NOT COME (R-rc9-5a). The three ways a workspace leaves a
           machine disagree deliberately — an archive carries restore points, a Save As copy carries
           nothing, a copy from a git address carries the versions and not the safety net — and a
           designer who is not told will assume the strongest of the three. It is stated BEFORE the
           button rather than reported afterwards, because "that is what I expected" and "why has my
           history gone" are the same sentence at two different moments.

      There is deliberately NO credential field. circuitRF holds no sign-in and asks for none (§9.1):
      it uses whatever git on this machine is already set up with. If the address needs one that git
      cannot supply, the copy REFUSES with a sentence naming what was wanted — it never hangs waiting,
      which is the one failure mode with no message, no exit code and no end.
    -->

    <Grid RowDefinitions="*,Auto" Margin="20,16,20,16">

        <StackPanel Grid.Row="0" Spacing="10">

            <TextBlock FontSize="13" FontWeight="SemiBold" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       Text="Copy a workspace onto this machine and open it"/>

            <TextBlock FontSize="12" TextWrapping="Wrap" Opacity="0.85"
                       Text="Give the address the workspace's maintainer gave you, and say where to put it. What arrives is an ordinary workspace: you can open it, read it, reference its cells, and — if you are allowed to — send your own changes back."/>

            <TextBlock FontSize="12" Margin="0,6,0,0" Text="Address"/>
            <TextBox x:Name="SourceBox" FontSize="12" Watermark="the address you were given"/>

            <TextBlock FontSize="12" Margin="0,6,0,0" Text="Put it in"/>
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto">
                <TextBox Grid.Column="0" x:Name="DestinationBox" FontSize="12"
                         Watermark="a folder that does not exist yet"/>
                <Button Grid.Column="1" x:Name="BrowseButton" FontSize="12" Padding="10,4"
                        Margin="8,0,0,0" Content="Browse…" Click="OnBrowseClick"/>
            </Grid>

            <TextBlock x:Name="WhatComesLabel"
                       FontSize="11" TextWrapping="Wrap" Opacity="0.7" Margin="0,8,0,0"/>

            <TextBlock x:Name="StatusLabel"
                       FontSize="11" TextWrapping="Wrap" Opacity="0.85"/>
        </StackPanel>

        <StackPanel Grid.Row="1" Orientation="Horizontal" HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                    Spacing="8" Margin="0,16,0,0">
            <Button x:Name="CancelButton" FontSize="12" Padding="10,4"
                    IsCancel="True" Content="Cancel" Click="OnCancelClick"/>
            <Button x:Name="CopyButton" FontSize="12" Padding="10,4"
                    IsDefault="True" Content="Copy" Click="OnCopyClick"/>
        </StackPanel>
    </Grid>
</Window>
